  :adr/body
  "## Question

This repo's decoder is full Clojure on the JVM. The destination
(com-junkawasaki/root ADR-2607198300) is an artifact that needs no JVM, Node
or Rust at run time. The question this ADR answers is narrow and answerable:
is the path open at all? Can a real, bit-exactness-critical piece of H.264
decode arithmetic be written in guest grammar, compiled through the JDK-free
front door, EXECUTED, and shown to agree with the .cljc decoder on data that
came out of a real bitstream?

## What was built

`src/h264/transform.kotoba` and `src/h264/quant.kotoba`, beside the untouched
`.cljc` files, exactly as `syntax.kotoba` sits beside `syntax.cljc`. Between
them they carry the whole 8.5.9 dequant chain and the 8.5.12 inverse
transform's arithmetic.

## Why the exported unit is the 1-D operator and not `inverse-4x4`

Not taste. Sixteen coefficients cannot cross an exported boundary today. A
`:vector-i64` parameter is rejected by the native backends; sixteen scalar
parameters exceed `guest-grammar.edn`'s `:max-parameters 5`. What can cross is
four coefficients and an output index.

That is not a mutilation of the spec. H.264's 2-D inverse transform is
separable and IS DEFINED as two passes of the 1-D butterfly, so the 1-D
operator is the standard's own primitive. The host performs the eight calls
and the index permutation and performs no arithmetic of its own: the +32 DC
bias lives in `idct4-1d-dc` and the final `>>6` in `idct4-1d-scaled`
specifically so that nothing arithmetic leaks back out to the caller. The
`.cljc`'s internal `transpose16` is absorbed into the gather, which is why
pass 1 reads the coefficient ROWS.

## Evidence

Three executions — the `kotoba.kir` reference interpreter, the emitted wasm32
module under node, and the emitted aarch64 machine code under amu's W^X kexe
loader — drive the same composition and are compared against the same corpus.
`java`, `javac`, `clojure` and `clj` are shadowed on PATH by stubs that exit
127; the run fails if the marker they write ever appears, so 'no JVM was used'
is asserted, not assumed.

The corpus is not invented. Every expectation comes from this repo's own
`.cljc` decoder, and every one of the 317 inverse-transform inputs is a
coefficient vector the decoder actually passed to `inverse-4x4` while decoding
five real libx264 fixtures. `test/h264/kotoba_kernel_vectors_test.cljk` is the
half the verifier cannot do: it re-derives every expectation from the current
`.cljc` code, re-decodes the fixtures to prove every recorded block really
came out of a bitstream, and refuses a corpus too degenerate to discriminate.
That last part matters — a corpus of flat blocks would pass an inverse
transform that ignored every AC coefficient, and a corpus with no negative
coefficients would pass a truncating division in place of H.264's arithmetic
right shift, which is the single easiest way to get this kernel subtly wrong.

And because a comparison that cannot fail is decoration,
`scripts/verify-kotoba-kernel-controls.cljk` breaks the guest kernels one edit
at a time in a throwaway copy and requires the verifier to reject each broken
copy IN THE GROUP THE EDIT IS IN. That last clause is not pedantry: the first
time the controls ran, one of them went red for a different check than the one
it had broken, and the run had to be treated as unproven until the verifier
learned to print every failing group rather than the first three samples.

Counts and per-path outcomes are in the measurement file rather than here, so
they can be re-checked rather than quoted.

## The emitted Wasm has zero host imports, and that was a design constraint

676 bytes for `transform`, 1,336 for `quant`, no imports at all. Keeping every
export scalar is what buys that. A `:vector-i64` or record parameter compiles
for wasm too, but pulls in the 45-function `kotoba:typed` import surface, and
the module stops being self-contained. The verifier fails the run if the
import count ever stops being zero, because the claim being made is 'runs with
nothing underneath it' and that claim has a number attached to it.

## What this does NOT prove

One transform kernel running JVM-free does not make the decoder JVM-free, and
the remaining distance is not small. A profile of this decoder -- taken
previously and NOT re-measured by this work, so the three figures are an order
of magnitude and not a result of this ADR -- attributes about 78% of leaf
samples to Clojure persistent-collection machinery and about 85% of
decoder-attributed samples to picture assembly and residual addition. The
transform is 3.4%.

What remains is precisely the part this kernel's shape cannot reach. Picture
assembly moves planes; a plane is bulk data; the guest value model has no
carrier for bulk data. A 256x256 luma plane exactly exhausts the native arena,
320x240 does not fit, and 1920x1080 exceeds it thirtyfold. So the honest
reading is: the path is open, and what is left is a value-model problem rather
than a translation problem. Anyone quoting this ADR as 'the decoder is moving
off the JVM' is quoting 3.4% of it.

## The one measurement that points at the next step

The native backends reject a `:vector-i64` parameter and accept a
SIXTEEN-FIELD `:i64` record in the same position, at arity 2. A whole 4x4
coefficient block can therefore reach a native export today. What it costs is
the Wasm side's zero-import property. That trade — not the five-parameter
limit — is the next real decision for widening this kernel to the 2-D
transform in one call, and it should be made with a round-trip measurement
rather than by preference.

## Compiler blockers met on the way

Minimal reproductions with exact invocations, exit codes and full diagnostic
maps are checked in under `test/h264/kotoba-compiler-repro/`, indexed by
`reproductions.edn`, so the compiler owners can act on them rather than
re-derive them. The one that shaped the code is that `bit-shift-left`,
`bit-shift-right`, `unsigned-bit-shift-right`, `mod`, `rem`, `min`, `max` and
`/` have no admitted lowering even though `guest-grammar.edn`'s
`:admitted-builtins` lists all eight; H.264 is specified in arithmetic shifts,
so every shift here is spelled as floor division or as multiplication by a
power of two, exactly and deliberately, with the reason written next to it."}]
